From d731e9bb463214806312bf0d68c98978289ed7d7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Volker Badziong <development@richtfest.tech>
Date: Tue, 21 Jul 2026 03:59:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 6/9] Stage per-server scope properties across server startup

A game server's load-time peak routinely dwarfs its steady working set --
world generation and asset streaming reach for everything at once -- so a
memory.high sized for the steady state throttles exactly the phase that can
least afford it. Worse, the damage is permanent: memory.min and memory.low
only fend off reclaim driven from OUTSIDE the cgroup, so reclaim a cgroup
inflicts on itself by exceeding its own memory.high ignores them entirely. A
ceiling below the load-time peak evicts the server straight through its own
floor, and those pages come back only as the workload faults them in again.

This stages a separate startup memory band (startup_defaults /
WINGS_CG_STARTUP_*) that holds until the server reports ready -- the egg's
own startup matcher, or WINGS_CG_STEADY_MATCH where "done" fires before the
world has finished loading -- or until startup_grace expires, whichever comes
first. The steady ceiling is then reached by a self-pacing ramp
(steady_ramp_step) that walks memory.high down and pauses at each step until
reclaim catches up, instead of slamming it in one shot. Only the memory band
is staged: cpu/io weights are work-conserving and cannot evict anything.
WINGS_CG_PHASE_EVENTS optionally mirrors named console lines into the Panel
activity log.

Adapted for scope-targeted properties. The startup band used to be applied in
OnBeforeStart, just before the container started, because its slice already
existed by then. A container scope does not exist until the container's init
process is launched, so the band is applied immediately after
ContainerStart() instead -- the same property sets, one step later.

Two consequences for the property applier itself. It now takes the band to
apply, so the panel-side in-situ re-assertion added with the scope work picks
one: startup while the environment is still in the starting state, steady
otherwise -- the same signal the phase machinery keys on, read from the layer
that actually has it. And it takes whether the steady ramp may run, because
the ramp belongs to the startup->steady handoff, where the ceiling genuinely
drops below a load-time peak; a re-assertion writes the value already on the
unit, so there is nothing to walk and a reclaim-paced loop would sit on the
caller's path for nothing.

The phase no longer pins a unit. Pinning existed because placement was fixed
at container-create time, so a Panel sync changing WINGS_CGROUP_PARENT
mid-startup could leave the steady band on a slice the container was not in.
The scope name is now read back from the container that actually exists at
apply time, so it cannot drift; what remains is a plain in-progress flag.

